(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physical-diagnosis-2-exam-1-pd2-exam-1-master-exam-form-d"]={"title":"PD2 Exam 1 Master Exam — Form D","category":"Physical Diagnosis","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"An elevated, circumscribed, encapsulated lesion in the dermis or subcutaneous layer filled with liquid or semisolid material is called what?","choices":["A nodule","A pustule","A cyst","A bulla"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the sebaceous cyst is the example given, and encapsulation is the distinguishing feature. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"Which tuning fork is used for the hearing tests, and under what conditions?","choices":["A 128 hertz fork — the larger one — in a quiet room","A 512 hertz fork, with background noise to mask the other ear","Any available fork, since only the comparison matters","A 512 hertz fork — the smaller one — in a quiet room"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The frequency matters because it sits in the speech range and rings long enough to compare two positions. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"What is the Rinne result in CONDUCTIVE hearing loss, and why?","choices":["Bone conduction equal to or greater than air conduction","Air conduction greater than bone conduction","Neither is heard at all","Bone conduction is heard but air conduction is heard longer"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The bone route goes round the obstruction and reaches an intact cochlea directly. Because vibration through bone bypasses the blocked external or middle ear. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"What happens to vision and the pupil in conjunctivitis?","choices":["Vision is normal but the pupil is fixed and mid-dilated","Vision is decreased and the cornea is steamy","Vision is not affected except for mild blurring from discharge","Vision is usually decreased and the pupil is irregular"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — preserved vision and a normal pupil are what make it benign. The pupil is not affected. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"When should the lacrimal sac compression test be avoided?","choices":["If the patient is over sixty-five","If the area is significantly inflamed or tender","If the patient wears contact lenses","If the patient has had cataract surgery"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — it is to avoid it in that situation. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"What is the rule on abbreviations in written clinical assignments?","choices":["Abbreviations are encouraged wherever they shorten the note","No abbreviations","Abbreviations are acceptable once defined on first use","Only widely recognised medical abbreviations may be used"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and it is stated without qualification. — Intro to PD II - Elwaya .pdf, Page 20"},{"q":"What additional finding appears in CONGENITAL Horner syndrome?","choices":["Bilateral ptosis","Proptosis of the affected eye","The involved iris is lighter in colour","The involved iris is darker in colour"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the material notes this for the congenital form. Heterochromia. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 87"},{"q":"A circumscribed, flat discoloration measuring less than one centimetre is called what?","choices":["A patch","A papule","A plaque","A macule"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — a freckle is the classic example. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"Which causes of hoarseness should a history cover?","choices":["Viral laryngitis","Allergic rhinitis, sinusitis and postnasal drip","Gingivitis and dental caries","Presbycusis and tinnitus"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — a list that runs from the trivial to the serious, which is why hoarseness always earns a full history. Voice overuse, laryngeal nerve damage, reflux and smoking. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"Which cause of red eye shows redness that is DIFFUSE and maximal peripherally, with vision unaffected?","choices":["Acute iritis","Glaucoma","Corneal injury or infection","Conjunctivitis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — diffuse peripheral redness with preserved vision is the conjunctivitis column. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 48 (image only)"},{"q":"A soft tissue infection around the cuticle or nail fold that is painful and purulent is called what?","choices":["Nail pitting","Acute paronychia","Onycholysis","Subungual hematoma"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — pain and purulence distinguish the acute form from the chronic one. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 92"},{"q":"A TENDER thyroid suggests what?","choices":["A solitary cyst","Thyroiditis","Graves disease","Endemic goitre"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Diffuse enlargement splits three ways on texture alone: soft is Graves, firm is Hashimoto, and tender is thyroiditis. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 88"},{"q":"What is the vital sign of the eye?","choices":["Intraocular pressure","Pupil reactivity","Visual fields","Visual acuity"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the material labels acuity testing the vital sign of the eye. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"What advice is given about using notes while presenting a case?","choices":["Read from them verbatim so that no detail is omitted","Notes are not permitted during a case presentation at all","Hand the notes to the listener rather than presenting aloud","Try not to read from them"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — reading works directly against helping the listener visualise the patient. — Intro to PD II - Elwaya .pdf, Page 9"},{"q":"Which complaints fall in the EMERGENT group, referred to ophthalmology immediately?","choices":["Blepharitis, chalazion and dry eye","Conjunctivitis and subconjunctival haemorrhage","Sudden vision loss, retinal artery occlusion, chemical burns, rupture, acute angle-closure glaucoma, and vitreous haemorrhage","Acute glaucoma, orbital cellulitis, corneal ulcer or abrasion, retinal detachment, macular oedema, and hyphaema"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the emergent list, which Beck asked the class to know. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 124"},{"q":"At what age do conductive and sensorineural hearing loss typically begin?","choices":["Both typically begin after sixty","Both typically begin in childhood","Conductive in childhood and young adulthood up to about forty","Conductive in middle or later years; sensorineural in childhood"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and the split is useful because it puts the prior probability in place before you touch a tuning fork. Sensorineural in middle or later years. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"Which term describes grouped papules or vesicles arranged as they are in a herpes simplex infection?","choices":["Zosteriform","Annular","Confluent","Herpetiform"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the configuration is named for the infection it resembles. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A chalky white patch on the tympanic membrane of a patient with a history of grommets. What is it, and does it matter?","choices":["Tympanosclerosis","Tympanosclerosis — and it always requires referral","A cholesteatoma — and it requires urgent referral","A healing perforation — which will close spontaneously"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It records past disease rather than present disease, which is why recognising it saves an unnecessary work-up. Hyaline deposit, usually not clinically significant. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Which two causes account for excessive tearing or dryness?","choices":["Corneal abrasion and foreign body","Obstruction in the lacrimal apparatus","Conjunctivitis and blepharitis","Cataract and glaucoma"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — a mechanical cause and an immune one. Sjögren syndrome. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Which findings characterise a subconjunctival haemorrhage on examination?","choices":["Pain present with photophobia and a hazy cornea","Purulent discharge with lid crusting","An irregular pupil with ciliary flush","Pain absent, vision and pupil unaffected, no discharge, cornea clear"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the four negatives, which are what make it benign. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"A headache of SUDDEN, SEVERE onset should raise which diagnosis?","choices":["Migraine","Cluster headache","Subarachnoid haemorrhage","Tension headache"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Sudden and severe is the pattern that cannot be attributed to a benign cause without investigation. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 92"},{"q":"Which term describes twisted, coiled or spiral lesions?","choices":["Gyrate","Arciform","Reticular","Discrete"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — gyrate lesions have a snakelike, coiled quality. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"What is the swinging light test for, and what is its indication?","choices":["Alignment of the visual axes; indicated for diplopia","Refractive error; indicated for blurred vision","Intraocular pressure; indicated for headache","Functional impairment of the optic nerve and the integrity of the visual pathways; indicated for anisocoria"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the purpose and indication. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"What distinguishes a splinter hemorrhage from a subungual hematoma?","choices":["A splinter hemorrhage is painful while a subungual hematoma is painless","A splinter hemorrhage affects all nails while a subungual hematoma affects one","A splinter hemorrhage is a transverse line while a subungual hematoma is longitudinal","A splinter hemorrhage arises from the distal capillary loop"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the source of the bleeding is what separates the two findings. While a subungual hematoma is hemorrhage into the nail plate. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 89"},{"q":"Which equipment should be available for a skin examination?","choices":["A ruler and a light source only","A dermatoscope and a Wood's lamp","A tape measure and a skin marking pen","A ruler, a light source, a magnifying lens"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The ruler matters because several morphological definitions turn on whether a lesion exceeds one centimetre. Gloves for any open lesions. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A temporal field defect in one eye should prompt what?","choices":["Testing for a nasal defect in the other eye","Immediate fundoscopy of the same eye only","Repeating acuity in the same eye","Measuring intraocular pressure"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the material asks you to test that possibility explicitly. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"How is a corneal scar distinguished from a cataract on examination?","choices":["The scar is painful; the cataract is not","The scar affects vision more than the cataract","The scar is a superficial greyish-white corneal opacity","The scar is visible only through the pupil; the cataract is superficial"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the material warns explicitly not to confuse them. The cataract lies on a deeper plane and is visible only through the pupil. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 92"},{"q":"What is a NORMAL Rinne result, and what is the reasoning behind it?","choices":["Both heard for exactly the same duration","Neither heard, in a quiet room","Air conduction heard longer than bone conduction","Bone conduction heard longer than air conduction"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The intact conducting pathway is more efficient than transmitting through bone. Because a sound beside the ear is louder than one against the skull. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"Which scalp and skin conditions are looked for during the head examination?","choices":["Only naevi and keratoses","Lice, seborrhoeic dermatitis, psoriasis, atypical naevi and actinic keratosis","Only alopecia and scarring","Only lice"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The scalp is easy to skip and carries both infestation and premalignant change. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 93"},{"q":"What should be reviewed before submitting a written clinical assignment?","choices":["The grading rubric","A classmate's completed version of the same assignment","The academic calendar and the course schedule","Only the textbook chapter on documentation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — prior feedback is the most direct guide to what still needs improving. The comments left on previous assignments. — Intro to PD II - Elwaya .pdf, Page 20"},{"q":"Which characteristics of the skin are assessed by inspection, palpation or both?","choices":["Colour and lesions only, since the skin examination is essentially visual","Temperature and turgor only, since colour varies too much between patients","Thickness, elasticity and capillary refill time","Colour, moisture, temperature, texture, mobility and turgor, and lesions"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — six characteristics, of which colour and lesions begin with inspection and the remainder require touch. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"How does the course of CENTRAL vertigo differ in duration from the peripheral causes?","choices":["Always under one minute","Always several hours","Variable, but rarely continuous","Always continuous until treated"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the table's wording. It is a useful contrast because the peripheral causes each have a fairly characteristic duration. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"FINE hair and COARSE hair on examination point to which conditions?","choices":["Both to hypothyroidism","Both to nutritional deficiency","Fine hair to hyperthyroidism; coarse hair to hypothyroidism","Fine hair to hypothyroidism; coarse hair to hyperthyroidism"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — a small finding, but it is examined for at the same moment as the scalp and the skull. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 93"},{"q":"How much difference in pupil size is common, and when is anisocoria benign?","choices":["It is benign only if both pupils are under three millimetres","Half to one millimetre is common","Any difference at all is abnormal","Up to three millimetres is common"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the size difference alone does not make it abnormal. It is benign if the pupillary reactions are normal. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"A palpable, elevated solid mass measuring less than one centimetre is called what?","choices":["A papule","A macule","A nodule","A vesicle"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — nevi, warts and lichen planus are the examples given. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"What does the crescent shadow test with oblique lighting assess, and what does a shadow mean?","choices":["Pupil reactivity — a shadow means an afferent defect","The depth of the anterior chamber angle","Corneal clarity — a shadow means an epithelial defect","Lens opacity — a shadow means cataract"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the technique and its meaning. A shadow on the medial side means the iris is bowed forward, a narrow angle, and a raised risk of narrow-angle glaucoma. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 90"},{"q":"Which areas of the scalp are specifically named for inspection?","choices":["The vertex and the temples","The anterior hairline and the eyebrows","The crown alone, since disease elsewhere is rare","Behind the ears and the occiput"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — these are the areas most easily missed if the hair is not parted. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"What does the cover-uncover test reveal?","choices":["A field defect","A slight muscle imbalance not otherwise seen","An afferent pupillary defect","A refractive error"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the stated purpose. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"Which piece of equipment aids inspection when lice are suspected?","choices":["A tongue depressor","A magnifying glass","A Wood's lamp","A dermatoscope"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — nits appear as tiny white ovoid granules adherent to the hair shafts. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 82"},{"q":"Deep eye pain is associated with which condition?","choices":["Blepharitis","Acute narrow angle glaucoma","Allergic conjunctivitis","Corneal abrasion"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — it is deep pain to this. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"How does the SIZE of the striking object change the injury?","choices":["Larger objects strike the globe directly; smaller objects hit the rim","Object size makes no difference","Only velocity matters, not size","Larger objects transfer most of the energy to the orbital rim"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the mechanical distinction. Smaller objects may strike the globe directly. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 111"},{"q":"What is inspected on the mucosa, tonsils and pharynx?","choices":["Erythema, exudates, ulcerations and lesions","Only exudates","Only the size of the tonsils","Only the colour of the mucosa"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the four things being looked for every time, whatever the complaint. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"A patient says they are “dizzy”. Which four categories must the history separate?","choices":["Vertigo, presyncope, disequilibrium and psychiatric causes","Vertigo, migraine, seizure and syncope","Peripheral vertigo, central vertigo, tinnitus and hearing loss","Orthostatic, cardiac, neurological and metabolic"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The word means nothing until the patient explains it, and these are the four things it turns out to mean. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"What is geographic tongue, and what does it mean?","choices":["A fungal infection of the tongue","A benign map-like pattern of patches","A premalignant lesion requiring biopsy","A sign of vitamin B12 deficiency"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Recognising it matters mainly so it is not mistaken for something that needs investigation. Of no pathological significance. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"The tongue deviates to one side on protrusion. Which nerve is involved?","choices":["Cranial nerve XII","Cranial nerve X","Cranial nerve V","Cranial nerve VII"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Asymmetric protrusion is the hypoglossal sign, and it is why the tongue is checked for midline position. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"What is tinnitus, by definition?","choices":["A perception of rotation or spinning","Hearing loss confined to the high frequencies","A sensation of fullness or pressure in the ear","Sound perceived with no external source"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The definition turns on the absence of an external stimulus, which is what separates it from any complaint about external sound. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Round scaling patches of alopecia with hairs broken off close to the scalp describe which infection?","choices":["Tinea capitis","Alopecia areata","Trichotillomania","Androgenic alopecia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the broken hairs distinguish it from other causes of patchy hair loss. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"A palpable, elevated solid mass greater than two centimetres is called what?","choices":["A cyst","A tumor","A plaque","A nodule"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — neoplasms are the example given. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"How is the kinetic red target test performed?","choices":["Wiggle the fingers and bring them into view","Ask the patient to count fingers at each quadrant","Move a 5 mm red-topped pin inward from beyond the boundary of each quadrant along a line bisecting the meridians, and ask when the pin first appears RED","Move a white target inward and ask when it is first seen at all"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — including the detail that the endpoint is when it first appears red. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"Which failure would make an infectious red eye worse?","choices":["Failing to recognise an infectious cause and inappropriately prescribing topical corticosteroid drops","Failing to measure the intraocular pressure","Failing to dilate the pupil","Failing to document the family history"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — it is this among its common mistakes. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Which of the following is a function of the skin?","choices":["Production of vitamin D","Production of clotting factors","Storage of calcium for release into the blood","Filtration of nitrogenous waste from the blood"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The skin also protects internal structures, prevents entry of microorganisms, regulates temperature and participates in excretion. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"When asking about medications in a dermatological history, what must be included?","choices":["Only medications applied directly to the affected skin","Only medications started within the past week","Both over-the-counter and prescription systemic medications","Only prescription medications, since these are the ones documented in the chart"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — limiting the question to prescriptions misses a large share of drug eruptions. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"On the red-eye chart, what is the PUPIL like in glaucoma?","choices":["May be small and, with time, irregular","Not affected unless iritis develops","Dilated and fixed","Not affected"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the chart's glaucoma column, and a cardinal danger sign. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 48 (image only)"},{"q":"How are the frontal and maxillary sinuses palpated?","choices":["Press UP on the frontal sinuses while avoiding the eyes","Press DOWN on the frontal sinuses and down on the maxillary sinuses","Press directly on the globes to assess the frontal sinuses","Percuss both sinuses with a reflex hammer"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Both are pressed upward, and the caution about the eyes matters because the frontal sinus sits immediately above the orbit. UP on the maxillary sinuses. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"Which term describes a ring-shaped lesion?","choices":["Annular","Arciform","Reticular","Gyrate"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — annular means shaped like a ring. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"What does bleeding of the gums suggest?","choices":["Necrotising ulcerative gingivitis only","Oral carcinoma","Gingivitis","Vitamin B12 deficiency"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Bleeding gums point to gingivitis — inflammation of the gum margin itself. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"Fine, irregular blood vessels that blanch under pressure are called what?","choices":["Spider angiomas","Cherry angiomas","Telangiectasias","Petechiae"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — blanching confirms the blood remains within vessels. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"What does the term confluent mean when applied to skin lesions?","choices":["The lesions form a line or stripe","The lesions run together","The lesions remain separate from one another","The lesions form a cluster"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the opposite of discrete, where lesions remain separate. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"An enlarged supraclavicular node on the LEFT. What does it suggest?","choices":["Local infection of the scalp","Dental infection","Tonsillitis","Metastasis from an abdominal or thoracic malignancy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The left supraclavicular node drains territory far from the neck, so finding one there redirects the whole search. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"What distinguishes a nodule from a papule?","choices":["A nodule is transient while a papule persists","A nodule is deeper in the dermis and measures one to two centimetres","A nodule is fluid-filled while a papule is solid","A nodule is flat while a papule is elevated"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — depth as well as size separates the two, and the examples given are basal cell carcinoma and neurofibromatosis. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 36"}]};
